      },
      {
        "text": "Do you ask what those wild beasts are? They are wrath, despondency, envy, strife, slanders, accusations, falsehood, hypocrisy, intrigues … love of praise, desire of honor (which indeed most of all drives the human soul headlong to perdition) … contempt of the poor, paying court to the rich.",
        "topics": [
          "passions",
          "humility",
          "sin"
        ],
        "source": "John Chrysostom, On the Priesthood III.9, NPNF1 vol. 9. https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/19223.htm"
      },
      {
        "text": "At such a time angels stand by the Priest; and the whole sanctuary, and the space round about the altar, is filled with the powers of heaven, in honor of Him who lies thereon.",
        "topics": [
          "prayer",
          "devotion"
        ],
        "source": "John Chrysostom, On the Priesthood VI.4, NPNF1 vol. 9. https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/19226.htm"
      },
      {
        "text": "I do not think there are many among Bishops that will be saved, but many more that perish: and the reason is, that it is an affair that requires a great mind.",
        "topics": [
          "humility",
          "sin"
        ],
        "source": "John Chrysostom, Homilies on Acts, Hom. 3 (section not confirmed), NPNF1 vol. 11. https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/210103.htm"
      },
      {
        "text": "I will now try and unveil to you the storm of my soul",
        "topics": [
          "humility",
          "truth"
        ],
        "source": "John Chrysostom, On the Priesthood, Book VI, NPNF1 vol. 9 (section not confirmed). https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/19226.htm"
      },
      {
        "text": "I had many genuine and true friends, men who understood the laws of friendship, and faithfully observed them",
        "topics": [
          "love"
        ],
        "source": "John Chrysostom, On the Priesthood I.1, NPNF1 vol. 9. https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/19221.htm"
      },
      {
        "text": "The present season is one for tears, and not for words; for lamentation, not for discourse; for prayer, not for preaching.",
        "topics": [
          "prayer",
          "suffering"
        ],
        "source": "John Chrysostom, Homilies on the Statues, Hom. 2.1 (Antioch, 387), NPNF1 vol. 9. https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/190102.htm"
      },
      {
        "text": "'Vanity of vanities, all is vanity'—it is always seasonable to utter this but more especially at the present time.",
        "topics": [
          "wisdom",
          "humility"
        ],
        "source": "John Chrysostom, Homily I on Eutropius, sec. 1, NPNF1 vol. 9. https://www.ccel.org/ccel/schaff/npnf109.xv.iii.html"
      },
      {
        "text": "My wounds work health, but their kisses have produced an incurable disease.",
        "topics": [
          "truth",
          "love"
        ],
        "source": "John Chrysostom, Homily I on Eutropius, sec. 1, NPNF1 vol. 9. https://www.ccel.org/ccel/schaff/npnf109.xv.iii.html"
      },
      {
        "text": "And the Church which you treated as an enemy has opened her bosom and received thee into it",
        "topics": [
          "mercy",
          "forgiveness"
        ],
        "source": "John Chrysostom, Homily I on Eutropius, sec. 1, NPNF1 vol. 9. https://www.ccel.org/ccel/schaff/npnf109.xv.iii.html"
      },
      {
        "text": "And as the fire makes the piece of gold, when it is applied to it, of better proof: so also affliction when it visits golden characters renders them purer and more proven.",
        "topics": [
          "suffering",
          "patience"
        ],
        "source": "John Chrysostom, Letter to Olympias (exile, 404–407), NPNF1 vol. 9. https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/1916.htm"
      },
      {
        "text": "Why do you lament? Why do you belabour yourself, and demand of yourself a punishment which your enemies were not able to demand from you, having thus abandoned your soul to the tyranny of dejection?",
        "topics": [
          "hope",
          "peace"
        ],
        "source": "John Chrysostom, Letter to Olympias (from Cucusus), NPNF1 vol. 9. https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/1916.htm"
      },
      {
        "text": "For perhaps it seemed good to God that I should be set to run the longer double course, in order that the garland of victory might be rendered more glorious.",
        "topics": [
          "courage",
          "patience"
        ],
        "source": "John Chrysostom, Letter to Olympias (from Cucusus), NPNF1 vol. 9. https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/1916.htm"
      },
      {
        "text": "Wherefore also I beseech your Honour to rejoice at these things, to be glad, and leap for joy, and to glorify God who has counted me worthy to suffer such things.",
        "topics": [
          "joy",
          "suffering"
        ],
        "source": "John Chrysostom, Letter to Olympias (after Caesarea, 404), NPNF1 vol. 9. https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/1916.htm"
      },
      {
        "text": "Come now let me relieve the wound of your despondency",
        "topics": [
          "hope"
        ],
        "source": "John Chrysostom, Letter to Olympias (exile), opening, NPNF1 vol. 9. https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/1916.htm"
      },
      {
        "text": "Do not be anxious on my behalf, nor rack yourself with solicitude, on account of the severity of the winter, and the weakness of my digestion, and the incursions of the Isaurians.",
        "topics": [
          "peace",
          "love"
        ],
        "source": "John Chrysostom, Letter to Olympias (from Cucusus), NPNF1 vol. 9. https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/1916.htm"
      },
      {
        "text": "during the last two months I have been no better than one dead, nay worse.",
        "topics": [
          "suffering"
        ],
        "source": "John Chrysostom, Letter to Olympias (from Cucusus), NPNF1 vol. 9. https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/1916.htm"
      },
      {
        "text": "Approach, although you may have repented a thousand times.",
        "topics": [
          "mercy",
          "forgiveness"
        ],
        "source": "John Chrysostom, as quoted by Socrates, Ecclesiastical History VI.21, NPNF2 vol. 2. https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/26016.htm"
      },
      {
        "text": "Attempt, sir, no such promise, nor order what is holy to be given to the dogs.",
        "topics": [
          "courage",
          "truth"
        ],
        "source": "John Chrysostom to the emperor Arcadius, in Theodoret, Ecclesiastical History V.32, NPNF2 vol. 3. https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/27025.htm"
      },
      {
        "text": "I have received this Church … from God our Saviour, for the care of the salvation of the people, and I cannot desert it",
        "topics": [
          "courage"
        ],
        "source": "John Chrysostom to the emperor, Lent 404, in Palladius, Dialogue ch. 9, tr. Moore (1921). https://tertullian.org/fathers/palladius_dialogus_02_text.htm"
      },
      {
        "text": "Did ye give praise to what has been said? Nay, I want not applause, nor tumults, nor noise. One thing only do I wish, that quietly and intelligently listening, you should do what is said. This is the applause, this the panegyric for me.",
        "topics": [
          "humility",
          "truth"
        ],
        "source": "John Chrysostom, Homilies on Matthew, Hom. 17, NPNF1 vol. 10. https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/200117.htm"
      },
      {
        "text": "This place is a spiritual school.",
        "topics": [
          "wisdom",
          "devotion"
        ],
        "source": "John Chrysostom, Homilies on Matthew, Hom. 17, NPNF1 vol. 10. https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/200117.htm"
      },
      {
        "text": "Then the houses were churches, but now the church has become a house.",
        "topics": [
          "devotion"
        ],
        "source": "John Chrysostom, Homilies on Matthew, Hom. 32, NPNF1 vol. 10. https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/200132.htm"
      },
      {
        "text": "When I look on that throne, deserted and bereft of our teacher, I rejoice and weep at the same time.",
        "topics": [
          "love",
          "suffering"
        ],
        "source": "John Chrysostom, Homilies on the Statues, Hom. 3.1 (Antioch, 387), NPNF1 vol. 9. https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/190103.htm"
      },
      {
        "text": "our city has become all at once a monastery.",
        "topics": [
          "devotion",
          "courage"
        ],
        "source": "John Chrysostom, Homilies on the Statues, Hom. 17.8 (Antioch, 387), on the monks who came down from the mountains, NPNF1 vol. 9. https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/190117.htm"
      },
      {
        "text": "I saw the swords and I meditated on Heaven; I expected death, and I bethought me of the resurrection.",
        "topics": [
          "courage",
          "faith"
        ],
        "source": "John Chrysostom, Homily II on Eutropius, sec. 2, NPNF1 vol. 9. https://www.ccel.org/ccel/schaff/npnf109.xv.iv.html"
      },
      {
        "text": "I fear not an enemy's plots: one thing only do I fear, which is sin.",
        "topics": [
          "sin",
          "courage"
        ],
        "source": "John Chrysostom, Homily II on Eutropius, sec. 4, NPNF1 vol. 9. https://www.ccel.org/ccel/schaff/npnf109.xv.iv.html"
      },
      {
        "text": "Was I not continually telling thee that wealth was a runaway?",
        "topics": [
          "wisdom"
        ],
        "source": "John Chrysostom, Homily I on Eutropius, NPNF1 vol. 9. https://www.ccel.org/ccel/schaff/npnf109.xv.iii.html"
      },
      {
        "text": "sin is the only thing which is really distressing; and that all other things are but dust and smoke.",
        "topics": [
          "sin",
          "peace"
        ],
        "source": "John Chrysostom, Letter to Olympias (exile, 404), NPNF1 vol. 9. https://www.ccel.org/ccel/schaff/npnf109.xvii.vi.html"
      },
      {
        "text": "Is it the desolation of this place which grieves you? Yet what can be pleasanter than my sojourn here?",
        "topics": [
          "joy",
          "suffering"
        ],
        "source": "John Chrysostom, Letter to Olympias (exile, 404), NPNF1 vol. 9. https://www.ccel.org/ccel/schaff/npnf109.xvii.vi.html"
      },
      {
        "text": "Do not then now desire death, nor neglect the means of cure.",
        "topics": [
          "hope",
          "love"
        ],
        "source": "John Chrysostom, Letter to Olympias (from Cucusus), NPNF1 vol. 9. https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/1916.htm"
      },
      {
        "text": "I am not surprised that you call my long letter a short one. For this is just the way with lovers",
        "topics": [
          "love"
        ],
        "source": "John Chrysostom, Letter to Castus, Valerius, Diophantus, Cyriacus and others (exile), NPNF1 vol. 9. https://www.ccel.org/ccel/schaff/npnf109.xvii.viii.html"
      },
      {
        "text": "Scythians and Sarmatians would never have decided a case after hearing one side only",
        "topics": [
          "justice",
          "truth"
        ],
        "source": "John Chrysostom to Pope Innocent I (404), preserved only in Palladius, Dialogue ch. 2, tr. Moore (1921). https://tertullian.org/fathers/palladius_dialogus_02_text.htm"
      },
      {
        "text": "When day came, the whole city moved outside the walls, and kept the feast under trees and thickets, like sheep scattered abroad.",
        "topics": [
          "suffering",
          "devotion"
        ],
        "source": "John Chrysostom to Pope Innocent I, on Easter 404, preserved only in Palladius, Dialogue ch. 2, tr. Moore (1921). https://tertullian.org/fathers/palladius_dialogus_02_text.htm"
      },
      {
        "text": "Every one will expel his neighbour, and be expelled in turn.",
        "topics": [
          "justice",
          "unity"
        ],
        "source": "John Chrysostom to Pope Innocent I (404), preserved only in Palladius, Dialogue ch. 2, tr. Moore (1921). https://tertullian.org/fathers/palladius_dialogus_02_text.htm"
      }
